\n","| Email | [190300395@ucaribe.edu.mx](mailto:190300395@ucaribe.edu.mx) |\n","| GitHub | [github.com/LuisBarahonaMX](https://github.com/LuisBarahonaMX) |\n","| LinkedIn | [linkedin.com/in/luis-barahona-mx](https://www.linkedin.com/in/luis-barahona-mx/) |\n","| Instagram | [@luisbarahonamx](https://www.instagram.com/luisbarahonamx/) |\n","| X | [@luisbarahonaMX](https://x.com/luisbarahonaMX) |\n","| Website | [SIKO AI Platform](https://sikoai.web.app/) |\n","</div>\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"hxvbnx294dpN"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["# Hospital Mortality Analysis and Predictive Modeling Using Machine Learning\n","\n","### Exploratory Data Analysis, Data Visualization, and Predictive Classification with Python\n","\n","**Field:** Data Science | Artificial Intelligence | Healthcare Analytics  \n","**Programming Language:** Python  \n","**Environment:** Google Colab | Jupyter Notebook\n","\n","---\n","\n","## Project Description\n","\n","This notebook presents an exploratory analysis of historical hospital mortality records using a dataset containing **1,121 observations**, including years, ICD-10 classifications, diseases, and patient counts.\n","\n","The main objective is to identify the leading causes of recorded hospital mortality, examine historical trends across different periods, and explore the application of Machine Learning algorithms to identify records associated with high patient counts.\n","\n","## Objectives\n","\n","- Perform data cleaning, transformation, and exploratory data analysis (EDA).\n","- Identify diseases associated with the highest recorded patient counts.\n","- Compare mortality patterns before the COVID-19 pandemic (2013–2019), during the pandemic (2020–2022), and in 2023.\n","- Develop statistical visualizations and interactive charts.\n","- Encode categorical variables using LabelEncoder.\n","- Implement a classification model using XGBoost.\n","- Evaluate model performance using Accuracy and Classification Reports.\n","- Explore experimental predictions for the year following the last available record.\n","\n","## Methodology\n","\n","**1. Data Exploration:** Import Excel datasets, examine records, and identify missing values.\n","\n","**2. Data Transformation:** Clean and standardize disease names and related classifications.\n","\n","**3. Temporal Analysis:** Compare major mortality categories across three historical periods.\n","\n","**4. Data Visualization:** Create bar charts, heatmaps, and three-dimensional visualizations to identify patterns and trends.\n","\n","**5. Machine Learning:** Construct a classification target based on the 75th percentile of patient counts, train an XGBoost model, and evaluate its performance using a test dataset.\n","\n","**6. Experimental Prediction:** Generate exploratory estimates for the following year and visualize categories with a higher predicted probability of exceeding the established threshold.\n","\n","## Technologies Used\n","\n","**Python | Pandas | Matplotlib | Seaborn | Plotly | Scikit-learn | XGBoost | Google Colab**\n","\n","## Potential Applications\n","\n","This project explores how Data Science and Machine Learning can support healthcare data analysis by identifying historical patterns, improving data visualization, and developing experimental predictive classification models.\n","\n","The methodology provides a foundation for further research into healthcare analytics, hospital data management, and data-driven decision support systems.\n","\n","## Scope and Limitations\n","\n","The classification model predicts whether a record exceeds a statistical threshold based on patient counts. It does not directly predict individual deaths or represent a clinically validated mortality prediction system.\n","\n","Future predictions require additional temporal validation and evaluation using new datasets before they can be considered for real-world healthcare applications.\n","\n","---\n","\n","**Author:** Data Engineer Luis Barahona  \n","**I